<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vijaya (Champa)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Đồ Bàn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vijaya was a region and capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binh-dinh-province">Bình Định province</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. From around the 11th century it was the chief royal centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> kings, with the citadel of Đồ Bàn and many brick towers. The Vietnamese emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a> captured and sacked it in 1471, breaking Champa's power, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> later made the area their base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vijaya-champa.jpg" alt="Vijaya (Champa)" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam,_shiva,_da_thap_banh_it_(torre_d%27argento),_stile_di_transiz._tra_my_son_A1_e_thap_mam,_Xi-Xii_sec,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vijaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for victorious and also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Vijayapura</a>, lay in the lowlands along the lower Côn River, in the south of what is now Bình Định Province, at modern An Nhơn. Near the river's estuary a well-protected harbour gave rise to Thị Nại, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>'s main ports, while the river into the western highlands carried trade with the hill peoples, who supplied luxury goods such as eaglewood for export. One of the larger rivers of Champa also made its soils more fertile than those of many other places.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was probably one of the earliest landfalls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> people in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, but its buildings suggest that it rose to importance only in the 11th or 12th century, and the earliest mention of Vijaya as a Cham city dates from about 1153 to 1184. In the 12th and 13th centuries it was drawn into wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> subdued it in 1145 and deposed Jaya Indravarman III, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> were defeated in 1149, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> at times dominated the city.</p><p>A Yuan army under the Mongol commander Sogetu took Vijaya early in 1283; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> were driven out, but the city was sacked. A Vietnamese army besieged it without success in the Battle of Vijaya in 1377, and in 1403 Vietnamese troops besieged the citadel for a month before a lack of food forced them back. The final attack in early 1471 came after almost 70 years without major fighting, and it is read as a response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>'s request for Chinese help against Đại Việt. Vijaya was destroyed, while the southern principalities kept a status like that of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Unlike most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> buildings, which used brick alone, Vijaya's combined stone and brick, which suggests influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> and a larger supply of labour. Its style was long dominant, and Cham architecture of the 12th to 14th centuries is classed as the Bình Định style. Many of its towers survive, among them the Cánh Tiên tower in the ruined citadel and the Dương Long towers, and the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> era Hoang De citadel lie within the old walls.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vijaya (Champa)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1778 Nguyen Nhac proclaimed himself emperor at Do Ban, near the old Cham capital of <b>Vijaya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
